WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.875
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:06.720
Hello, how are we doing?

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.770
Excellent, very good.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.750
I am thrilled to be here and
I'm thrilled to be talking about the topic

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:15.420
of how to make our
communication memorable.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.310
So today is all about specific tips and
tricks that you can use so that you can

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.590
help yourself remember what your trying to
say, and to help your audience remember.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.900
While i like communicating,
I love hearing you communicate as well.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.490
So today is going to be participative, and
i invite you to participate as much, or

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:36.900
as little as you like.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.130
So let's go ahead and get started, and
you'll see by some of the imagery that I'm

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:44.180
using, I'm trying to keep
a very serious subject playful.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.890
So when I ask my students or the people
I coach, what worries you the most?

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.320
What concerns you the most?

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.960
I hear two things all the time.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.060
I'm afraid I'm not going to
remember what I need to say.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.740
Followed immediately by,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.820
I'm afraid my audience isn't
going to remember what I say.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.030
So today is all about trying to
give those tools that we can use

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.980
to make sure that our
communication is memorable,

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:18.190
and we'll start by looking at how we can
help ourselves remember our content.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:19.410
And then we'll move forward and

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.820
we'll talk about how to make sure
that your content is memorable.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.050
And we'll start by talking
about presentation hygiene.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:33.729
To me, presentation hygiene is about what
you do to prepare for your communication.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.470
Not the actual practice,
which we will talk about, but

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.550
specifically what you do in your
life to get yourself ready.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.110
So to begin,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:48.030
one of the most important things we
can do is make sure that we eat well.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.280
I coached an individual who kept getting
promoted in his organization, and

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.280
he noticed that as he got promoted, he got
more nervous, and more forgetful when he

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:03.490
gave presentations and random meetings,
and I asked him, why do you think that is?

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.290
And he had no idea.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.660
And I said walk me through your day.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.430
And what we learned was,

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:13.310
as he kept getting promoted,
his diet got worse and worse.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.210
He was drinking more caffeine,
he was eating more fast food, and

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.410
he couldn't remember well.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.980
By looking into some nutritional advice,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:27.440
by eating more healthy, he actually
regained his confidence and his memory.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.390
Preparing for a presentation or an
important meeting, like you would prepare

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.200
for some kind of athletic event,
is exactly what you should do.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:41.550
Make sure that you treat
your diet well and

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.250
that you focus on elements
like what you eat, and

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.620
when you eat certain foods to
make sure you're prepared.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.250
Quick bit for advice, caffeine while
preparing your communication but

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.970
not before you deliver your communication.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.340
In addition to eating, we also have
to make sure that we exercise.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.820
We have to make sure that
our body is warmed up, and

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.070
it works very well to
reduce stress that we have.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.510
So instead of spending a whole night
cramming for that big meeting or

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.740
that big presentation,
it's far better to do a little exercise,

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.620
to breath deeply, and
get a good night's sleep.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.030
Sleep is critical to memory formation and

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:28.110
to making sure you have the energy
you need to communicate effectively.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:33.050
So simply hygiene, what you eat,
the exercise you do,

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:34.920
the sleep you get, can really help.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.270
Now, we live in a valley of technology.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:44.950
So part of our hygiene in preparation
needs to focus on technology.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:50.020
There are myriad of apps and
tools that you can use to help yourself

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.470
become a better,
more effective communicator.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.870
I'm going to list several, here.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:00.210
These are tools that range from helping
you reduce the number of disfluencies you

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.650
have, those are the ums,
uhs, likes and I means.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.410
All the way through tools that help you
with your vocal intensity and your flow.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.780
One of these tools actually
allows you to don VR goggles, and

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.960
see an imaginary audience.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.480
An audience you can determine if it's
friendly or hostile, big or small.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.980
You can even see your slide deck
behind you in a virtual world.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.380
Leverage tools to help you
with your preparation.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.970
This is the first step to being
an effective, memorable communicator.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:42.800
Next, we actually have to think about
the messages that we communicate, and

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.148
I believe all effective communication,

00:04:46.148 --> 00:04:50.780
all high-stakes communication,
must start with a goal.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.920
And to me,
a goal has three key components.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.880
It's about information,
emotion, and action.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.610
At the end of your meeting,
at the end of your presentation,

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.750
at the end of your email,
what do you want your audience to know?

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.230
How do you want them to feel,
and what do you want them to do?

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:22.108
You should be able to articulate your goal
clearly before you create your content.

00:05:22.108 --> 00:05:24.513
My goal for us today is simple.

00:05:24.513 --> 00:05:28.035
I'd like for each and every one of
you to leave here with some tools and

00:05:28.035 --> 00:05:30.720
tactics you can use to be more memorable.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.290
I want you to feel confident, and
dare I say excited to try these tools, and

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.660
ultimately, I'd love for you to use them.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.360
Each of you needs to think
about what is your goal for

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.030
your upcoming high stakes communication.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:50.680
And by the way, having a goal not
only helps you focus what you say,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.420
it also helps you assess
the success of your communication.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.470
In the work I do with my students, here,
and the consulting I do around the world,

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:05.170
I often ask people, how do you know
if your communication was successful?

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.770
And the most frequent answer I get is,
I got through it.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:16.280
Survival becomes the success criteria,
and that's not where we want to be.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.180
If you have a goal, you can actually
assess were you successful.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.170
Do the people know what
I wanted them to know?

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:24.390
Are they feeling the way I want
them to feel, and ultimately,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.180
are they doing what I wanted?

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.670
So having a goal helps you
not only hone your message,

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.160
it helps you assess its success.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.750
So once we have that goal,

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.250
we then need to think about how do
we best communicate our message?

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.900
And I am a huge proponent of structure.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.840
Structure is critical to
success in communication.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.680
Structure helps us in two ways.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.770
First, it helps us to set our
audience's expectations, and

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.450
second, it helps our audience to remember.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:05.340
I learned this first rule
very early in my life.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.260
When I was an undergraduate here
at Stanford, I was a tour guide.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.295
They trained me for 12 weeks of full
academic quarter to be a tour guide.

00:07:14.295 --> 00:07:16.640
To this day, I can still work
backwards in a straight line.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:24.040
What do you think was the most
important thing they taught us

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.870
during that full quarter in terms
of being a good tour guide here?

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:28.450
What do you think?

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.760
Shout out some of the things.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.890
&gt;&gt; Smile.
&gt;&gt; Smile, What else?

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.150
&gt;&gt; Make people feel welcome.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:35.230
&gt;&gt; Make people feel welcome.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.200
What else?

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:36.700
&gt;&gt; Project.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.320
&gt;&gt; Project, be nice.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.970
The best thing, listen, the best and

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.613
most important thing they said to be a
good tour guide is never lose your group.

00:07:45.613 --> 00:07:48.018
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:48.018 --> 00:07:51.408
&gt;&gt; You are a bad tour guide if you lose
your tour group, and here on campus,

00:07:51.408 --> 00:07:54.928
they were afraid of people get lost,
they might get hurt.

00:07:54.928 --> 00:07:57.710
The same thing is true
in your communication.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.700
Never lose your audience,
because once you lose,

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.035
them it's very hard to get them back.

00:08:02.035 --> 00:08:02.810
They go to their phones.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.450
They go to their friends.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.040
They go to sleep.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.480
We need to keep them with us.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.460
Setting expectations helps.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.010
Would you join me on my tour if I showed
up and I said, hi, I'm Matt, let's go?

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.660
Couple of you adventurers folks would
come but the rest of you be like no way.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:20.490
Where are we going?

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.220
Why are we going there?

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:23.140
Do I have the right shoes on?

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.970
That's why tour guides always set
expectations that structure helps prepare

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.910
you so you can be present and
enjoy the tour.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.450
Structuring your communication
does the same thing, but

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:38.660
structure also helps with memory for
you and for your audience.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:42.470
It provides a map, and
you can't be lost if you have a map.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:48.130
So allow me to share with you
three of my favorite structures.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.630
That once you have your goal
you place your content into.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:58.080
The first structure is a comparison
contrast conclusion structure.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.390
You list things in common,
you list things that are different, and

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:04.240
then you identify your
particular conclusion.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:11.680
Another structure that works very well is
the problem solution benefit structure.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.800
You start by talking about
what the problem is.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.794
You then talk about how you solve it,
and ultimately,

00:09:17.794 --> 00:09:20.607
you end with the benefits
to the person listening.

00:09:20.607 --> 00:09:24.932
Now, sometimes the problem isn't really
a problem, it's really an opportunity.

00:09:24.932 --> 00:09:30.002
So this could become the opportunity
solution benefit structure as well.

00:09:31.684 --> 00:09:36.620
Now finally, my most favorite structure,
and I'm the kind of guy that has

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:41.171
a favorite structure, is the what,
so what, now what structure.

00:09:41.171 --> 00:09:44.810
In this structure, you start by defining
what it is you're talking about.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.520
A product, a process, an idea.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.910
You then talk about why it's important
to the people you're talking to.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:54.800
That's the so what, and
then the now what are the next steps.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:56.970
What can somebody do
with that information?

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.470
I like this structure so much because
it not only helps with presenting, but

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:05.520
it's a great way to set
up a meeting agenda.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.520
It's a good way to structure an email.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.510
You can even give feedback and
answer questions in this structure.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:13.600
I encourage each and

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.080
every one of you to try putting your
communication into this structure.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:21.920
Now, I'm smiling and
I'm going to take a time out.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.490
This is a meta moment for us.

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:30.380
I just used what, so what, now what
to explain what, so what, now what.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.840
I told you what it was, why it was
important, and how you can use it.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:38.800
Take these structures or
any structure you find beneficial, and

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.900
leverage it to make your communication
memorable for you and your audience.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.620
We start with a goal and
then we put it in a structure.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.490
All right, enough of me talking.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.822
We're going to put this all together.

00:10:52.822 --> 00:10:55.250
I'm going to ask you to find
a partner in one moment, and

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.530
I'm going to task you to do something
with one of these structures.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.110
So to begin, how many of you remember
what this device is up here?

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.920
It's a slinky, a children's toy,
whose sole purpose, I think,

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.850
is to walk down stairs.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.160
Except in my house, it never worked.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:16.380
You are going to sell a slinky to your
partner, and then your partner will sell

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:22.520
the very same slinky back to you using
the problem solution benefit structure.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:23.440
Identify a problem.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.820
Explain the solution and
explain the benefit.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:29.030
Now, one caveat.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.560
This cannot be a children's toy.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:36.690
You have to make it up
to be something else.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.780
I never ask my students to do
something I'm not willing to do, so

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:41.930
allow me to demonstrate.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:48.740
Have you all heard of a terrible
plight affecting the African Savanna?

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.030
Day after day,
week after week elephants are dying.

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:54.890
Why, you ask?

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:57.925
Because their trunks are getting cold.

00:11:57.925 --> 00:12:01.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Allow me to introduce you to

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:04.625
an expandable elephant trunk warmer.

00:12:04.625 --> 00:12:08.120
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; With these cheap very effective

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.450
supplies and tools,

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:15.280
you can reduce the deaths of
elephants on the African Savannah.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.466
Each of us must do our part.

00:12:17.466 --> 00:12:21.202
Problem, solution, benefit.

00:12:21.202 --> 00:12:23.080
Here's what you're going to do.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.000
You're going to find a partner.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.090
For some of you,
it's just turn to the person next of you.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.170
A few of you might need
to move around the room.

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:36.790
You have one minute each to sell
your device to your partner.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:38.860
Ready, begin.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.330
So I'm very curious.

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:43.300
I'm very curious.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.580
What did this device become for
some of you?

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:46.110
Raise your hand.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.010
I'll call on you.
What was this?

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:48.660
&gt;&gt; A cup holder.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:49.400
&gt;&gt; A cup holder.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.320
What was it back there?

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:52.290
&gt;&gt; For electric cords.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:56.730
You put your cords in one section.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:57.940
You can stretch it around the room and
then-

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.800
&gt;&gt; It's a koozie for electric cords.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.550
Yes, sir, please.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:02.820
&gt;&gt; An egg timer.
&gt;&gt; An egg timer.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.130
The egg sort of rolls down.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:04.770
Yes, please.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.514
&gt;&gt; A peaceful way to track gophers.

00:13:07.514 --> 00:13:10.670
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] A politically
correct way to trap gophers.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:11.170
Yes, sir.

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.800
&gt;&gt; It's an exercise tool.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:16.375
In the back.

00:13:16.375 --> 00:13:18.190
&gt;&gt; A bagel slicer.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:19.240
&gt;&gt; A bagel slicer.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:21.540
Multiple bagel slicer, excellent.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.090
I don't know what you and
your partner came up with, but

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:28.020
in looking out at you,
you are having a lot of fun.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.290
The pantamiming was fantastic.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:35.740
Let me ask you this,
though, in all seriousness.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:40.860
Was the structure helpful in
your creating your pitch?

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.850
I see several of you nodding your head.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.410
In what ways did the structure help you?

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:46.148
What did it do for you?

00:13:47.832 --> 00:13:50.430
It helps you focus on the problem.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:51.510
What else does it do for you?

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:52.810
&gt;&gt; It helps you remember.

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:55.290
&gt;&gt; It helps you remember where
to go from one to the next.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.340
What else, the sequence?

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.580
&gt;&gt; Finish.
&gt;&gt; It helps you finish.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.420
In a lot of the teaching and
coaching I do, people tell me, Matt,

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.160
I don't have a problem communicating.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.320
I have a problem stopping communicating.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.230
So the structure actually
gives you a place to finish.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.800
After I say the benefits, I'm done.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.190
It also helps you move from
one place to the next.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:16.740
It has built in transitions.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.420
So the flow is easier.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:24.560
So structure helps in memory, but
it has a lot of other added benefits.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.160
So once we've taken care of
our personal hygiene, and

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.740
we then think about our goal and
our structure,

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:36.340
the next thing we have to do to help
with our own memory is practice.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:39.680
So I'd like to give you some advice and
guidance on practicing.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:45.399
First, when you practice
actually stand and speak.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:51.220
If you're in a meeting or you're preparing
for a presentation, stand up and practice.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.930
I know how most people practice.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.150
We open up our slide
deck that we've created.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.570
We sit and we look at each slide,
and we think to ourselves hm.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:01.480
That's what I'll say.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.380
Mm-hm, that's what I'll say.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.645
I don't know about you, but
in my mind, I'm amazingly eloquent.

00:15:06.645 --> 00:15:08.760
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But when I open up my mouth,

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:13.450
I'm not always as lucky, so it's useful
and practical to actually stand and

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:16.760
speak, and standing helps you focus.

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:22.790
When I teach my class here on effective
virtual communication, that's where you're

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:27.020
doing webinar or video conference,
I encourage all of my students to stand.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:28.920
Adjust the camera, and stand.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:30.070
It helps you focus.

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:33.180
It allows you to use your voice,
fully and completely.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.610
It is a very useful tool.

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.886
Beyond standing to practice,
we need to make sure we warm up.

00:15:39.886 --> 00:15:43.471
Most speakers, especially those who
are nervous, go inside themselves and

00:15:43.471 --> 00:15:47.126
they think, and they think, and they
worry, and we don't warm up our voice.

00:15:47.126 --> 00:15:51.440
So our first utterances aren't
necessarily our strongest.

00:15:52.549 --> 00:15:55.080
I believe warming up is critical.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:16:00.887
So I'm going to ask you to help me do the
world's quickest and easiest vocal warmer.

00:16:00.887 --> 00:16:04.599
We are simply going to say
two words a few times each.

00:16:04.599 --> 00:16:09.136
And those two words are tea
cup followed by hiccup.

00:16:09.136 --> 00:16:13.970
So I'm going to ask you to say
each word three times in a pair.

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:17.589
So it's going to be teacup, hiccup,
teacup, hiccup, teacup, hiccup.

00:16:17.589 --> 00:16:18.827
And we're all going to say it out loud.

00:16:18.827 --> 00:16:22.585
And I want you to notice where in your
mouth those two words get produced.

00:16:22.585 --> 00:16:25.009
With me, ready, begin.

00:16:25.009 --> 00:16:29.862
Teacup, hiccup, teacup,
hiccup, teacup, hiccup.

00:16:29.862 --> 00:16:32.989
And we have a couple of extra credit,
yes, yes, yes I know, we are here,

00:16:32.989 --> 00:16:35.625
it's Stanford Business School,
everybody does the extra.

00:16:35.625 --> 00:16:37.548
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When you say teacup,

00:16:37.548 --> 00:16:39.083
where in your mouth those that come from?

00:16:39.083 --> 00:16:39.796
Front top.

00:16:39.796 --> 00:16:42.138
When you say hiccup,
where does that come from?

00:16:42.138 --> 00:16:42.934
Back, lower.

00:16:42.934 --> 00:16:45.464
You've warmed up your whole voice.

00:16:45.464 --> 00:16:46.327
Very simple.

00:16:46.327 --> 00:16:49.287
Now, I'm not saying you get up in front
of your very important meeting and

00:16:49.287 --> 00:16:51.412
say one moment please,
teacup, teacup, teacup.

00:16:51.412 --> 00:16:53.206
You do this before you enter the room.

00:16:53.206 --> 00:16:56.378
I like to go around and
talk to people in my audiences,

00:16:56.378 --> 00:16:59.479
in my meetings I like to make
sure I get there early and

00:16:59.479 --> 00:17:03.093
talk to people as they come in,
that's my way of warming up.

00:17:03.093 --> 00:17:05.317
Make sure you're warmed up.

00:17:05.317 --> 00:17:07.784
For those of you who do athletics or
who exercise,

00:17:07.784 --> 00:17:09.501
you know warming up is important.

00:17:09.501 --> 00:17:12.102
The same is true with your communication.

00:17:12.102 --> 00:17:16.821
So we practice standing up,
we warm up our voice.

00:17:16.821 --> 00:17:22.175
Now, when we practice, there's some really
good research in learning theory and

00:17:22.175 --> 00:17:27.461
cognitive neuroscience that tells us
chunking your information is very useful.

00:17:27.461 --> 00:17:30.940
Take pieces of it and
practice each piece separately, and

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:33.148
then assemble it in different ways.

00:17:33.148 --> 00:17:36.979
So if you're giving a big presentation,
perhaps you practice the beginning,

00:17:36.979 --> 00:17:38.535
then the middle, then the end.

00:17:38.535 --> 00:17:41.651
And then the next time you do the middle,
the end, the beginning?

00:17:41.651 --> 00:17:46.159
That form of chunking helps
you to remember each part.

00:17:46.159 --> 00:17:50.033
And it helps you avoid being
in a particular pattern.

00:17:50.033 --> 00:17:55.741
Memorization actually works against you
when you are in a high stress situation.

00:17:55.741 --> 00:18:00.662
Because if you deviate from the script,
you're lost.

00:18:00.662 --> 00:18:04.223
So if you are very familiar with
what you're talking about and

00:18:04.223 --> 00:18:07.110
practicing in a chunked
manner helps you do that.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:10.362
It also helps you give
attention to the end.

00:18:10.362 --> 00:18:15.150
Most people because we're so nervous
about how we're going to start practice

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:17.552
the beginning a lot more than the rest.

00:18:17.552 --> 00:18:21.018
So, we figure, by the time I get to
the end I'll know how to finish.

00:18:21.018 --> 00:18:23.167
Do you know the most
common way meetings and

00:18:23.167 --> 00:18:25.380
presentations end in the corporate world?

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:28.694
I guess we're out of time and
somebody needs the room.

00:18:28.694 --> 00:18:29.619
Thanks.

00:18:29.619 --> 00:18:34.234
That is not the most effective
way to end a presentation, so

00:18:34.234 --> 00:18:40.066
you must practice each part, and
each part individually, very helpful.

00:18:40.066 --> 00:18:44.354
Now, research done not very far from here
in the psychology department several

00:18:44.354 --> 00:18:46.341
decades ago on the Stanford campus.

00:18:46.341 --> 00:18:50.689
They did a tremendous amount of
work on how where you actually

00:18:50.689 --> 00:18:54.963
learn something influences and
impacts how you recall it.

00:18:54.963 --> 00:18:59.120
So if you practice in a situation that
is similar to the situation in which

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:03.358
you will communicate, you are more
likely to remember the information.

00:19:04.387 --> 00:19:06.004
So any of you who are parents,

00:19:06.004 --> 00:19:09.827
who've instructed your children
when they're studying for a test or

00:19:09.827 --> 00:19:13.795
a quiz to study in a quiet place,
you're actually doing them a service.

00:19:13.795 --> 00:19:14.892
You're helping them.

00:19:14.892 --> 00:19:18.948
My children like to study with
lots of noise and devices, yet

00:19:18.948 --> 00:19:24.039
when they take their exams or have to
write their essays, it's in silence,

00:19:24.039 --> 00:19:28.196
that's actually causing a problem
in terms of their memory.

00:19:28.196 --> 00:19:32.387
So if possible, go into the venue
that you'll be presenting in,

00:19:32.387 --> 00:19:34.992
practice in there, that will help you.

00:19:34.992 --> 00:19:40.203
So there's some very specific
things we can do to help prepare.

00:19:40.203 --> 00:19:44.120
But what happens if we go blank?

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.022
What happens if we forget?

00:19:47.022 --> 00:19:51.118
This is the number one
fear people report to me.

00:19:51.118 --> 00:19:54.960
So let me give you some advice
on how to handle blanking out.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:58.230
First don't acknowledge it.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.710
Man I just forgot.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:04.374
Instead leverage two techniques
I'd like to suggest.

00:20:04.374 --> 00:20:11.389
First repeat what you just said, and
see if that gets you back on track.

00:20:11.389 --> 00:20:14.630
When you lose your keys
how do you find them?

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.402
You retrace your steps.

00:20:16.402 --> 00:20:18.420
Do the same in your presentations.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:22.424
For some reason, when we're presenting or
running meetings, and we blank out,

00:20:22.424 --> 00:20:26.094
all we do is think about all the things
that we need to do or say in the future.

00:20:26.094 --> 00:20:27.478
I only have ten more minutes left.

00:20:27.478 --> 00:20:28.519
This is really important.

00:20:28.519 --> 00:20:29.488
I have to get the funding.

00:20:29.488 --> 00:20:30.460
That's all future thinking.

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:34.239
You would never do that
if you lost your keys.

00:20:34.239 --> 00:20:36.892
If you lose your keys, you don't stand
there and say, I have to get to the store,

00:20:36.892 --> 00:20:38.688
I have to pick up the,
we don't talk about the future,

00:20:38.688 --> 00:20:39.922
we'd say where did I put those keys.

00:20:39.922 --> 00:20:40.860
We'd go back.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:42.350
Repeat what you just said.

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:47.183
Most of us can remember what we said
even if we can't remember what we intend

00:20:47.183 --> 00:20:47.731
to say.

00:20:47.731 --> 00:20:53.292
And simply by repeating yourself,
it often will get you back on track.

00:20:53.292 --> 00:20:56.289
Now if that doesn't work,
let me give you a second tool.

00:20:56.289 --> 00:20:59.238
And I'm going to let you in on
a little secret when I teach.

00:20:59.238 --> 00:21:01.193
And I have some former students here, so

00:21:01.193 --> 00:21:04.655
you're going to hear something you
heard when you were a student of mine.

00:21:04.655 --> 00:21:08.637
Have a back pocket question at the ready.

00:21:08.637 --> 00:21:13.636
So if you forget, simply pause and ask
your audience a question thereby giving

00:21:13.636 --> 00:21:18.727
yourself time to collect your thoughts,
check your notes, get back on track.

00:21:18.727 --> 00:21:23.748
Here's what I do in my lectures when I
can't remember what I need to say next.

00:21:23.748 --> 00:21:28.026
I'll simply pause and say, this is
a good place for us to take a pause, and

00:21:28.026 --> 00:21:32.169
I'd like for you to reflect on
something that we've just discussed and

00:21:32.169 --> 00:21:34.258
how it applies to you and your life.

00:21:34.258 --> 00:21:36.050
And do you know what my students do?

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:41.070
They pause and reflect on how their life
has changed based on this information.

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:41.913
And what am I doing?

00:21:41.913 --> 00:21:42.920
I'm thinking what's next?

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:43.826
Where do I go?

00:21:43.826 --> 00:21:45.218
I check my notes.

00:21:45.218 --> 00:21:48.758
Now, if I'm really lost I'll say,
turn to your partner next to you and

00:21:48.758 --> 00:21:50.301
tell them the impact that has.

00:21:50.301 --> 00:21:53.798
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And my students think, wow,

00:21:53.798 --> 00:21:54.870
Matt really cares.

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:57.953
And I do, but I'm also finding my place.

00:21:57.953 --> 00:22:02.243
I want each of you to think about for
the types of communication you do,

00:22:02.243 --> 00:22:05.591
what are some questions you
could ask midway through or

00:22:05.591 --> 00:22:09.110
perhaps towards the end or
even at the beginning, that.

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:13.200
Would engage your audience in reflection
and distract them from paying attention

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.413
to the fact that you don't know
where you are so you can find out.

00:22:16.413 --> 00:22:19.294
There are lots of types of
questions that you can ask.

00:22:19.294 --> 00:22:21.300
Have them at the ready.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:25.819
Simply by knowing that you can repeat
yourself, go back to go forward or that

00:22:25.819 --> 00:22:30.778
you have a back pocket question makes most
people feel comfortable and confident.

00:22:30.778 --> 00:22:34.162
When they present which therefore
makes them more able to

00:22:34.162 --> 00:22:38.895
get through their presentation and less
likely to forget what they want to say.

00:22:38.895 --> 00:22:44.269
So the next time you fear blanking out,
know you have tools to get you through it.

00:22:47.574 --> 00:22:54.023
So we've talked about what we can
do to help ourselves remember.

00:22:54.023 --> 00:22:55.683
We've talked about hygiene.

00:22:55.683 --> 00:23:00.363
That's what we eat, we exercise,
we sleep, we leverage technology.

00:23:00.363 --> 00:23:05.086
We've talked about how we prepare
by having a goal, know, feel, do,

00:23:05.086 --> 00:23:07.232
and then creating a structure.

00:23:07.232 --> 00:23:09.392
And then we've talked
about ways to practice.

00:23:09.392 --> 00:23:13.961
We stand up, we chunk information,
we practice in the real environment.

00:23:13.961 --> 00:23:16.349
I'd like now to flip the coin and

00:23:16.349 --> 00:23:21.396
I'd like to talk about what we can
do to help our audience remember.

00:23:21.396 --> 00:23:23.207
And this involved three things too.

00:23:23.207 --> 00:23:28.295
It involves using variation, it involves
making sure our content is relevant and

00:23:28.295 --> 00:23:31.849
finally how can we leverage
emotion to make ideas stick.

00:23:31.849 --> 00:23:34.463
So let's start.

00:23:34.463 --> 00:23:35.898
We're going to talk about variation.

00:23:35.898 --> 00:23:38.328
We're going to talk about
variety in several ways.

00:23:38.328 --> 00:23:41.010
First, we're going to talk
about variation in your voice.

00:23:43.669 --> 00:23:50.861
Evolution has designed humans to pay
attention to variety, to novelty.

00:23:50.861 --> 00:23:55.267
If something stays the same, We habituate.

00:23:55.267 --> 00:24:00.190
If I were to have talked like this for
my entire lecture you

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:05.432
would no longer be paying attention and
likely, be asleep.

00:24:05.432 --> 00:24:09.062
Variety is what keeps us focused.

00:24:09.062 --> 00:24:12.302
So you must put variation in your voice.

00:24:12.302 --> 00:24:13.995
How do you do that?

00:24:13.995 --> 00:24:16.624
Well, record yourself and listen.

00:24:16.624 --> 00:24:18.512
Do you have variation?

00:24:18.512 --> 00:24:23.475
If you notice that you don't have variety,
use emotive words.

00:24:23.475 --> 00:24:27.357
When we invoke emotive words,
we tend to reflect their meaning.

00:24:27.357 --> 00:24:30.649
So I might say this is
a really big challenge.

00:24:30.649 --> 00:24:32.660
I wouldn't say this is
a really big challenge.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:35.320
The words really and

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:40.648
big invite that kind of vocal emphasis.

00:24:40.648 --> 00:24:45.663
When you are practicing,
make sure that you extend and

00:24:45.663 --> 00:24:50.690
exaggerate the way you would
normally say something.

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:54.832
When we get nervous, we tense up and
our vocal cords, our muscles and

00:24:54.832 --> 00:24:55.982
they tense up too.

00:24:55.982 --> 00:25:00.391
So if my normal vocal range is here and
I get nervous it becomes here.

00:25:00.391 --> 00:25:04.291
So when I practice,
I purposely practice in an exaggerated way

00:25:04.291 --> 00:25:08.500
knowing that when I get nervous it
will come back to my normal range.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:14.502
Be mindful of your voice and
the variation in it.

00:25:14.502 --> 00:25:18.468
We must also use variation, but
before we get to the next point,

00:25:18.468 --> 00:25:20.091
we also have to practice.

00:25:20.091 --> 00:25:22.044
I would like all of you to
notice this word up here.

00:25:22.044 --> 00:25:23.932
What is the word.

00:25:23.932 --> 00:25:25.553
We're going to say,

00:25:25.553 --> 00:25:29.770
in a variety of ways to make sure
that we have variation in our voice.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:34.921
In a moment, I'm going to ask you to say
as if a heavy object dropped on your foot.

00:25:34.921 --> 00:25:38.390
You might say a naughty word after you
say but I want us all to practice.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.538
Okay, on the count of three,
something heavy dropped on your foot,

00:25:40.538 --> 00:25:41.176
I want you to say.

00:25:41.176 --> 00:25:42.425
Ready, one, two, three.

00:25:43.564 --> 00:25:46.574
&gt;&gt; That was great.
Now, you just saw something so

00:25:46.574 --> 00:25:48.226
adorable and cute.

00:25:48.226 --> 00:25:50.364
Ready, one, two, three.

00:25:51.688 --> 00:25:56.026
&gt;&gt; Now you've just made a terrible
mistake and you're really sorry,

00:25:56.026 --> 00:25:57.405
how would you say?

00:25:57.405 --> 00:25:59.000
Ready, one, two, three.

00:26:00.124 --> 00:26:03.016
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Look what you just did.

00:26:03.016 --> 00:26:05.256
You were able to take
a very simple word and

00:26:05.256 --> 00:26:08.777
you were able to convey different
meaning by varying your voice.

00:26:08.777 --> 00:26:12.743
Imagine what you could do
in complex sentences and

00:26:12.743 --> 00:26:15.865
in an entire presentation or meeting.

00:26:15.865 --> 00:26:20.405
Variety in your voice is
what makes it engaging.

00:26:20.405 --> 00:26:23.357
We also need to think about
variety in the evidence and

00:26:23.357 --> 00:26:25.664
support that we use when we communicate.

00:26:25.664 --> 00:26:30.578
There are lots of things we can do to
encourage people to believe what we're

00:26:30.578 --> 00:26:34.248
saying, to motivate them to change,
to educate them.

00:26:34.248 --> 00:26:37.274
One type of evidence we can use is data.

00:26:37.274 --> 00:26:41.180
We use lots and lots of data in our lives.

00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:44.841
When you use data,
make sure you provide context so

00:26:44.841 --> 00:26:47.661
that people understand what it means.

00:26:47.661 --> 00:26:50.682
But data is but
one type of evidence you could use.

00:26:50.682 --> 00:26:52.726
Another type of evidence
has to do with testimonies.

00:26:52.726 --> 00:26:55.890
Third party voices,
other people advocating or

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.904
supporting what it is you say.

00:26:57.904 --> 00:27:04.630
And then, finally, there are stories,
examples, use cases, these too add value.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:09.448
Here's the key,
you must use variety among these types.

00:27:09.448 --> 00:27:13.596
Most of us communicate using the type
that we are most comfortable with,

00:27:13.596 --> 00:27:14.417
what we like.

00:27:14.417 --> 00:27:17.583
If I'm a data person when I communicate,
I use a lot of data.

00:27:17.583 --> 00:27:20.352
If I'm a storyteller,
I tell lots of stories.

00:27:20.352 --> 00:27:23.672
The problem is my audience
isn't all like me.

00:27:23.672 --> 00:27:25.701
So I have to cast a wide net.

00:27:25.701 --> 00:27:28.957
I have to use variety
in the support I use.

00:27:28.957 --> 00:27:31.477
Maybe I give a testimony and
give some data.

00:27:31.477 --> 00:27:34.444
Maybe I put both the testimony and
the data in a story.

00:27:34.444 --> 00:27:38.697
This helps my audience understand and
remember my points.

00:27:38.697 --> 00:27:43.591
Use variety to help you
support your points.

00:27:43.591 --> 00:27:49.269
We also have to think about variety in
terms of the images and visuals we use.

00:27:49.269 --> 00:27:54.353
You'll notice that many of my visuals
are referring to children's play or

00:27:54.353 --> 00:27:55.896
toys, and they vary.

00:27:55.896 --> 00:27:58.855
The idea is to keep the audience engaged.

00:27:58.855 --> 00:28:04.084
If all you show people is bullet point
after bullet point after bullet point,

00:28:04.084 --> 00:28:05.619
they will habituate.

00:28:05.619 --> 00:28:07.464
Remember, bullets kill.

00:28:07.464 --> 00:28:09.880
Don't kill your audience
with bullet points.

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:13.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Use images.

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:19.850
Now variation is important but
so too is relevance.

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:24.530
You must make sure that your content is
meaningful and relevant to your audience.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:27.037
That means we have to do
some reconnaissance and

00:28:27.037 --> 00:28:29.510
reflection about who's in the room.

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:33.039
Who's listening to my communication,
who's reading my messages?

00:28:33.039 --> 00:28:36.948
And we must tailor our
messages to their needs.

00:28:36.948 --> 00:28:42.596
Well some of you will get upset to hear
me say this, you need to change and

00:28:42.596 --> 00:28:47.697
adapt your communication audience
by audience, time by time.

00:28:47.697 --> 00:28:49.790
Does that mean more work for you?

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:52.058
Yes, but does it mean that the impact and

00:28:52.058 --> 00:28:55.138
the relevance your message
will have will be greater?

00:28:55.138 --> 00:28:57.954
Yes, and that's why we do it.

00:28:57.954 --> 00:29:02.427
So we need to tailor our messages for
our audience in several factors.

00:29:02.427 --> 00:29:04.500
One, we have to first think
about their knowledge.

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.799
What do they know about
what we're speaking on?

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:09.390
We don't want to come in too high level.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:10.880
We don't want to get too deep.

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:12.720
We need to meet our
audience where they're at.

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:14.170
So you have to think about that.

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:16.280
You have to do some reflection.

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:18.460
You have to ask people
who are in the audience.

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:21.720
Talk to people who have spoken
to this audience before.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:23.510
And ask what's their knowledge level.

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:27.210
You must also think about
their expectations.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:28.960
What is it they expect from you?

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:35.260
And then choose to either conform to those
expectations or perhaps deviate from them.

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:41.850
And then, finally, you need to understand
what are your audience's attitudes?

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:45.220
Are they in favor of what you're
suggesting, are they agnostic, or

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:47.670
perhaps hesitant or resistant?

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:51.600
And with this information,
we can craft our messages and

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:54.890
hone them to be more relevant and
memorable.

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:55.850
I want to give you an example.

00:29:56.900 --> 00:29:59.860
Let's say you are presenting to
an audience that you know is a bit

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:02.660
hesitant or
resistant to what you're advocating for.

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:07.206
That is valuable information.

00:30:07.206 --> 00:30:11.170
I might change the way I
structure a message base on it.

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.770
So you know that problem solution
benefit structure we talked about.

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:17.407
If I have a hesitant or
resistant audience,

00:30:17.407 --> 00:30:21.695
it might be in my best interest to move
the benefits to be the first part.

00:30:21.695 --> 00:30:25.555
Start by talking about the benefits.

00:30:25.555 --> 00:30:30.110
Imagine what it would be like if we could,
or what if it was possible to.

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:33.880
Get your audience to agree
to that imagined future and

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:36.540
that reduces their resistance.

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:39.654
You then say, what's in the way
is this particular problem and

00:30:39.654 --> 00:30:40.903
here's how we solve it.

00:30:40.903 --> 00:30:45.608
Now I certainly can't guarantee that all
resistance and hesitation will disappear,

00:30:45.608 --> 00:30:48.508
but I can suggest that by
starting with the benefits,

00:30:48.508 --> 00:30:51.320
you put yourself in a better position.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:54.850
So understanding your audience,
their knowledge, their expectations,

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:59.950
their attitudes can influence and should
influence the way you communicate, so

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:04.129
that your audience will receive your
message better and remember it longer.

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:11.580
It's not enough just to tailor our
message we also have to make it engaging.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:14.710
And I'd like to suggest some ways
that we can get physical engagement

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:15.980
as well as mental engagement.

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:20.790
Physical engagement is to have
your audience do something.

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:24.710
You could pose a question,
rhetorical or a polling question.

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:27.940
You can show a video, provide a schematic.

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:31.110
if you're in the virtual world,
have them go to an external website.

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:33.100
Have them type something into the chat.

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:34.839
Get your audience involved.

00:31:34.839 --> 00:31:38.712
By having them do something, they are more
likely to engage with the content,

00:31:38.712 --> 00:31:41.840
which we know increases
the likelihood of their remembering.

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:45.890
But physical engagement is not enough.

00:31:45.890 --> 00:31:49.520
You also need mental engagement,
and there lots of ways to do that.

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:50.920
I already alluded to one.

00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:54.459
Use the word imagine, or
what if, or picture this.

00:31:54.459 --> 00:31:59.383
We know from research done Two doors down,
the disengages,

00:31:59.383 --> 00:32:03.620
areas of the brain associated
with attention and retention.

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:07.950
So if I can ask you to imagine or
picture this in your mind,

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:11.920
you're actually seeing it,
which will help you remember it.

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:13.960
Similarly, use analogies,

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:18.710
analogies connect what people know to what
they don't, this also makes it stick.

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:23.210
I talked about structuring a presentation
is like giving a tour, that's an analogy.

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:28.801
By engaging our audience physical and
mentally, they are much more likely to

00:32:28.801 --> 00:32:33.234
remember what we're saying and have a good
time while you're communicating with them.

00:32:35.743 --> 00:32:39.295
Finally, we must leverage emotion,
we have known for

00:32:39.295 --> 00:32:44.560
millennia that emotion is powerful and
engages people and helps them remember.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:49.290
So we need to find ways to
leverage emotion in what we say.

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:55.032
Part of that is how we say it,
bring emotion to your

00:32:55.032 --> 00:32:59.275
messages if it's congruent and
appropriate.

00:32:59.275 --> 00:33:02.560
Show that you have passion,
show that you have interest,

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:07.460
a wonderful technique that comes from
the world of theater and acting.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:12.380
If you are trying to convey something that
you want to be exciting for your audience,

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:17.190
think about the time you were excited
right before you deliver that message.

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:20.480
By getting yourself into
that physical emotion,

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:22.320
you will deliver it more
likely in that way.

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.430
Now, some of you are thinking,
I get it, emotion is important.

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:31.090
But I talk about things that don't have a
lot of emotion, I talk about technology or

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:33.460
science, there's not
a lot of emotion there.

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:38.720
And you might be right, but
the consequences and implications and

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:43.710
results of that science and
technology has potential emotion.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:45.430
If you're saving trees, saving time,

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:48.600
saving lives,
there's emotion you can tap into.

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:55.030
Take the time to find a way to bring
emotion into your communication,

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:58.910
it will help the audience
feel something and remember.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:03.670
So we've talked about several factors

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:07.050
that help make your
communication more memorable.

00:34:07.050 --> 00:34:10.980
We talked about hygiene, that's that
preparation, we talked about structure and

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:12.390
practice.

00:34:12.390 --> 00:34:15.710
We then talked about what you can do to
help your audience in terms of variation

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:17.980
and relevance and emotion.

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:21.330
These are the levers
that you have to pull,

00:34:21.330 --> 00:34:25.380
to make your communication
much more memorable.

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:31.780
Now I want to see how memorable what
we just did together is for you.

00:34:33.230 --> 00:34:38.560
So I'm going to ask you to think about
one thing of value that you learned today

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:40.390
in our time together.

00:34:40.390 --> 00:34:43.126
And I want you to turn
to a different partner,

00:34:43.126 --> 00:34:46.439
somebody perhaps who you
didn't work with last time.

00:34:46.439 --> 00:34:50.814
And I want you to explain that thing
of value using the what, so what,

00:34:50.814 --> 00:34:52.700
now what structure.

00:34:52.700 --> 00:34:57.410
So you're going to tell your
partner what the idea or tool or

00:34:57.410 --> 00:35:01.310
tactic is, you're going to tell them
why you think that is important.

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:04.550
And you're going to then tell
them how you will use it, and

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:06.690
then your partner will do the same for
you.

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:10.430
I want to know what you're carrying out
of here that will help your communication

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:11.700
be more memorable.

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:13.140
So find another partner,

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:18.040
pick one thing of value, share that
value in the what, so what, now what?

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:22.830
We often don't spend time reflecting
on how we communicate, we are so

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:27.640
fixated on just getting it done and
moving on that we don't take time.

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:32.390
The only way to get better at your
communication is through three things,

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:36.190
repetition, reflection, and feedback.

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:37.870
You need to give yourself the reps,

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:42.520
you need to give yourself opportunities
to communicate and try new ways.

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:45.900
Try new structures, try varying your
voice, try new ways of practice.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:51.480
Second, you must reflect and
think about what worked and

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:54.670
what didn't work,
what's that definition of insanity?

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:57.350
Doing the same thing over and
over again, expecting different results?

00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:00.020
We do that all the time
in our communication,

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:02.220
we need to think about what worked and
what didn't.

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:05.690
I challenge all of you who lead teams and
other people.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:09.200
At the end of your meetings or
presentations take 30 seconds and

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:13.260
deep breathe, what worked and
didn't work in terms of the communication.

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:16.570
Not the content but
how we actually communicated.

00:36:16.570 --> 00:36:21.149
That sends a very powerful message to
your teams that communication matters.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:27.010
And then finally, we need feedback,
find trusted others, friends, family,

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:29.850
coworkers who can give
you honest feedback.

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:33.780
Use digital recording as a tool
to give yourself feedback,

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:34.960
that's how we get better.

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:38.740
There are also tools and
resources available to you, and

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:41.100
I encourage you to take advantage of them.

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:45.790
Here you see a website that I curate
called No Freaking Speaking, and

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:48.610
this is a website that has lots
of free content from me and

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:50.140
from others you could take advantage of.

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:56.200
Stanford Continuing Studies is a hidden
gem that this campus provides,

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:59.430
if you live in the local community,
look at the classes they offer.

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:01.150
There are a tremendous
number of business and

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:05.480
communication classes,
they also have many virtual offerings.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:07.950
For those of you visiting
us from far away,

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:12.210
you can take webinar-based
classes from continuing studies.

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:16.420
And then finally, Toastmasters,
an international organization designed to

00:37:16.420 --> 00:37:20.250
help people feel more comfortable and
confident when they communicate.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:25.469
Take time to find avenues to learn and
practice these skills.

00:37:27.110 --> 00:37:31.860
It is my hope that you are leaving here
today with some specific tactics and

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:33.710
techniques you can use.

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:35.770
To help you be more memorable and

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:39.020
your messages to be more
memorable when you communicate.

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:40.640
Before I take your questions, and

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:44.820
I invite those questions,
I have two questions for you.

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:49.678
Did you learn something today that you
think can help you in your communication?

00:37:49.678 --> 00:37:50.318
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:50.318 --> 00:37:51.630
&gt;&gt; Did you have a little bit of fun?

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:52.200
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:53.930
&gt;&gt; Then I have done my job.

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:57.110
Now I would like to hear what questions
you have of me, there's some microphones

00:37:57.110 --> 00:38:00.550
in the back, people will get them to you,
they're coming.

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:03.070
We'll have here and
then I'll sit over there, yes, sir?

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:06.730
Thank you very much,
Brent, that was terrific.

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:07.250
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:12.380
&gt;&gt; Who is the greatest speaker,
in your mind, that's ever lived?

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:16.871
&gt;&gt; That is an incredibly hard question.

00:38:16.871 --> 00:38:17.941
&gt;&gt; Just what first comes to your mind.

00:38:17.941 --> 00:38:19.431
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:38:19.431 --> 00:38:20.460
&gt;&gt; Are you going to ask me a question?

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:21.370
because you look quite blank.

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:22.428
&gt;&gt; No, no, no.

00:38:22.428 --> 00:38:26.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not.

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:30.550
I have been so moved in the last year

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:33.820
by the teenagers who suffered
that great tragedy in Florida.

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:38.250
The shooting which was
just absolutely horrific,

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:44.010
those young people are among some of
the most eloquent speakers I have heard.

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:47.190
And they're not coached,
they're not trained, and in fact,

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:52.140
they have actively not sought that out,
they've rejected those who want to help.

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:55.720
So when I look at people who've had
an impact, people whose messages

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:59.000
are memorable, people who deploy some
of the techniques we've talked about.

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:03.510
I look to those teens, and to me,
that is so moving, thank you for

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:04.800
that question, yeah.

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:05.475
Please, over here.

00:39:05.475 --> 00:39:08.890
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; You mentioned about talking

00:39:08.890 --> 00:39:10.660
to an audience that's hostile, but

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:14.960
what about an audience that just
doesn't relate to you at all?

00:39:16.348 --> 00:39:20.270
Sometimes I have talked to a group of
students of color, and trying to figure

00:39:20.270 --> 00:39:24.530
out exactly how to say something
that they open their minds to hear.

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:28.440
And the second question that may be
related is, what's the role of humor?

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:31.890
&gt;&gt; Right, so
let me take the second question first,

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:36.155
humor is a wonderful way to engage
your audience, if you're funny.

00:39:36.155 --> 00:39:40.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Having a shared laugh

00:39:40.970 --> 00:39:44.320
is a great way to bring people together,
it engages them.

00:39:44.320 --> 00:39:48.000
The problem is humor is trucky,
it's not always funny,

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:52.510
it's culturally sensitive,
you have to be careful.

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:56.910
Here are my two rules for
humor, focus-group your jokes.

00:39:56.910 --> 00:40:01.076
If you intend to use some humor, talk to
people who are like people who will be in

00:40:01.076 --> 00:40:03.238
the audience and ask them is this funny?

00:40:03.238 --> 00:40:06.370
And most importantly,
listen to what they tell you.

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:10.406
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Second, use humor about yourself.

00:40:10.406 --> 00:40:13.637
Self-deprecating humor is the safest.

00:40:13.637 --> 00:40:18.475
Even though it might be tempting
to talk about politics, or

00:40:18.475 --> 00:40:23.334
geo issues, stay focused on yourself,
it's much safer.

00:40:23.334 --> 00:40:25.522
Now how do you relate to an audience?

00:40:25.522 --> 00:40:29.462
That can be hard, a lot of it has
to do with the work you do upfront,

00:40:29.462 --> 00:40:33.202
try to understand what motivates them,
what are the issues?

00:40:33.202 --> 00:40:34.921
Who are the speakers they listen to or
look for?

00:40:34.921 --> 00:40:36.747
What helps connect?

00:40:36.747 --> 00:40:39.147
And then engage in dialog, so

00:40:39.147 --> 00:40:43.670
it's not always about you talking,
get them to speak.

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:47.801
So some of the best ways to engage in
an audience that you feel is going

00:40:47.801 --> 00:40:50.921
to be disconnected from you,
is to ask questions.

00:40:50.921 --> 00:40:55.034
Have them talk to each other, become
a facilitator rather than a presenter or

00:40:55.034 --> 00:40:55.677
a teacher.

00:40:55.677 --> 00:40:58.181
And that's a way to build comradery,
build trust and

00:40:58.181 --> 00:40:59.973
to break down some of those barriers.

00:40:59.973 --> 00:41:02.264
Microphone is over here.

00:41:02.264 --> 00:41:03.131
&gt;&gt; Yes please.

00:41:03.131 --> 00:41:08.926
&gt;&gt; Thank you, those thoughts
are applicable to different cultures,

00:41:08.926 --> 00:41:14.122
I mean that directorate is applicable for
almost everyone?

00:41:14.122 --> 00:41:19.035
&gt;&gt; I believe that much of what we have
talked about today is very relevant across

00:41:19.035 --> 00:41:19.969
culturally.

00:41:19.969 --> 00:41:24.684
Clearly there are something you have to
think about in terms of expectations,

00:41:24.684 --> 00:41:27.767
norms, etc,
to factor into your communication.

00:41:27.767 --> 00:41:32.437
But I do believe having a clear goal,
having a structure will help figuring out

00:41:32.437 --> 00:41:37.558
how to engage your audience, will help now
that engagement might differ by culture.

00:41:37.558 --> 00:41:42.197
But having engagement is critical, so yes
I believe at the highest level everything

00:41:42.197 --> 00:41:44.565
we've talked about today spans culture.

00:41:44.565 --> 00:41:49.345
How that gets deployed and what that looks
like, we'll have to take the culture and

00:41:49.345 --> 00:41:51.200
context into account for sure.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.553
We've got some questions here and
a microphone is coming.

00:41:53.553 --> 00:41:57.225
&gt;&gt; So great for presentations.

00:41:57.225 --> 00:41:58.272
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:41:58.272 --> 00:42:02.322
&gt;&gt; But oftentimes we are at
our age coaching people.

00:42:02.322 --> 00:42:09.216
So how do you take that into a two minute
synopsis of how you can use your skills?

00:42:09.216 --> 00:42:13.185
Because I've seen conversations kind of
explode and you can't even control it.

00:42:13.185 --> 00:42:13.926
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:42:13.926 --> 00:42:16.268
&gt;&gt; So yeah, so
just whatever thoughts you have.

00:42:16.268 --> 00:42:20.828
So I believe with practice, you can get
very effective at quickly coming up with

00:42:20.828 --> 00:42:25.061
a goal for the interaction you're about
to have and then use a structure.

00:42:25.061 --> 00:42:28.871
In coaching situations where you
are giving feedback, I think the what, so

00:42:28.871 --> 00:42:30.964
what, now what structure is wonderful.

00:42:30.964 --> 00:42:34.916
What becomes the issue or
the behavior that you want to address, so

00:42:34.916 --> 00:42:36.822
what is why it is important, and

00:42:36.822 --> 00:42:41.282
the now what is the guidance that you
believe the person should undertake.

00:42:41.282 --> 00:42:44.347
So having that structure can keep
it tight, keep it focused, and

00:42:44.347 --> 00:42:45.304
make it memorable.

00:42:45.304 --> 00:42:49.785
But it takes practice to be comfortable
doing that in a spontaneous way.

00:42:49.785 --> 00:42:54.683
So you have to practice doing that type
of feedback several times before it

00:42:54.683 --> 00:42:58.396
becomes natural but
I do believe coming up with a goal and

00:42:58.396 --> 00:43:02.189
structuring it can be very,
very helpful for feedback.

00:43:02.189 --> 00:43:03.582
As well as answering questions.

00:43:03.582 --> 00:43:04.635
Yes, please.

00:43:04.635 --> 00:43:07.189
&gt;&gt; The most important take away for

00:43:07.189 --> 00:43:12.400
me is that before communication,
preparation is so important.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:17.250
But preparation needs time and
usually there's not enough time.

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:20.853
[LAUGH] And
you also suggest us to have good sleep.

00:43:20.853 --> 00:43:23.703
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] right.

00:43:23.703 --> 00:43:28.496
&gt;&gt; So how can we Trade-off between-
&gt;&gt; I wish I had the secret to

00:43:28.496 --> 00:43:31.210
how we could all get
more time in our lives.

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:36.781
What I suggest is, the more you practice
this, the more efficient you become at it.

00:43:36.781 --> 00:43:39.172
So, you can things more quickly.

00:43:39.172 --> 00:43:40.792
It also has to do with prioritization.

00:43:40.792 --> 00:43:44.158
Many of us think well, I'm not going to
sleep tonight because I'm going to drill

00:43:44.158 --> 00:43:46.276
and drill this big
presentation I have to give.

00:43:46.276 --> 00:43:50.012
In fact the research suggests getting
a good night sleep is far better for

00:43:50.012 --> 00:43:52.289
you than repetitive practice while tired.

00:43:52.289 --> 00:43:57.232
So part of it is prioritizing based
on what you know will be helpful and

00:43:57.232 --> 00:44:01.423
then the other part is just
becoming more efficient at it.

00:44:01.423 --> 00:44:04.589
The more effective and efficient
you are the quicker you can do it.

00:44:04.589 --> 00:44:06.417
But it is a trade off.

00:44:06.417 --> 00:44:08.397
You have to have that initial investment.

00:44:08.397 --> 00:44:10.837
I have a very simple heuristic I use.

00:44:10.837 --> 00:44:14.994
If I have a big presentation coming up or
meeting, whatever that date is,

00:44:14.994 --> 00:44:18.492
I take the time between today and
that date and I cut it in half.

00:44:18.492 --> 00:44:22.525
And that first half is preparation and
that second half is practice.

00:44:22.525 --> 00:44:26.360
Most of us sacrifice the practice piece,
we're working on our content up until

00:44:26.360 --> 00:44:30.365
the last minute because we think, because
I've worked on it, I'm going to be able to

00:44:30.365 --> 00:44:34.457
deliver it well, and everyone of you who
has ever known that knows that's not true.

00:44:34.457 --> 00:44:37.019
So, I just use that simple heuristic and
after you that for

00:44:37.019 --> 00:44:39.122
a while it becomes easier,
it becomes easier.

00:44:39.122 --> 00:44:40.024
Yes please.

00:44:40.024 --> 00:44:43.436
&gt;&gt; I have a comment and
a question for you.

00:44:43.436 --> 00:44:44.286
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:44:44.286 --> 00:44:47.678
&gt;&gt; The first one is, when I was in
the Army I learned the Army way of

00:44:47.678 --> 00:44:51.853
communicating which is tell them what
you're going to tell them, tell them,

00:44:51.853 --> 00:44:53.701
and tell them what you told them.

00:44:53.701 --> 00:44:57.014
And you followed that
almost to the letter.

00:44:57.014 --> 00:44:57.989
&gt;&gt; Well thank you.

00:44:57.989 --> 00:44:59.223
&gt;&gt; The question is,

00:44:59.223 --> 00:45:04.994
do you ever use the Gettysburg Address as
an example of persuasive communication?

00:45:04.994 --> 00:45:09.305
&gt;&gt; So I use the Gettysburg Address
in my classes in a different way.

00:45:09.305 --> 00:45:12.817
There is, and
I'm not remembering who did this.

00:45:12.817 --> 00:45:17.151
But somebody took the Gettysburg Address
and created a PowerPoint for it,

00:45:17.151 --> 00:45:18.264
the whole speech.

00:45:18.264 --> 00:45:19.298
There's seven slides.

00:45:19.298 --> 00:45:24.481
And I use it to prove a point
that message is important,

00:45:24.481 --> 00:45:27.194
slides are less important.

00:45:27.194 --> 00:45:30.886
There's a bar chart, four score, right?

00:45:30.886 --> 00:45:35.365
It's very funny, but it proves a point and
you can all go search.

00:45:35.365 --> 00:45:37.590
Go search Gettysburg Address
as a PowerPoint and

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:39.008
you will see the seven slides.

00:45:39.008 --> 00:45:42.026
So I use it, but
I use it not as a persuasive tool,

00:45:42.026 --> 00:45:44.396
although it is a very effective tool.

00:45:44.396 --> 00:45:49.395
I use it to prove the point that we need
to have our story and message first,

00:45:49.395 --> 00:45:51.101
and then create slides.

00:45:51.101 --> 00:45:55.133
If we rely too heavily
on slides we lose a lot.

00:45:55.133 --> 00:45:58.252
And that makes that point very very well.

00:45:58.252 --> 00:46:00.206
Other questions, yes.

00:46:00.206 --> 00:46:04.034
&gt;&gt; I have a class that I have
to give in Galway in Ireland,

00:46:04.034 --> 00:46:06.087
of Executive MBA students.

00:46:06.087 --> 00:46:06.771
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:06.771 --> 00:46:08.886
&gt;&gt; And they're a wonderful,
wonderful group, but

00:46:08.886 --> 00:46:10.304
you know what the Irish are like.

00:46:10.304 --> 00:46:11.954
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And occasionally,

00:46:11.954 --> 00:46:13.392
&gt;&gt; The Irish are very friendly, yes.

00:46:13.392 --> 00:46:16.054
&gt;&gt; They are, they're fantastic especially
after the class is over in the pub.

00:46:16.054 --> 00:46:17.765
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Excellent.

00:46:17.765 --> 00:46:18.649
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:46:18.649 --> 00:46:22.458
&gt;&gt; But occasionally I have a student or
two that will start looking at

00:46:22.458 --> 00:46:27.304
their mobile phone during my piece, which
I suppose is partly criticism of me, but

00:46:27.304 --> 00:46:30.230
the others don't so
it isn't a total criticism.

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:34.217
How do you handle individuals,
or do you just ignore them,

00:46:34.217 --> 00:46:37.422
who appear to be distracted
by something else?

00:46:37.422 --> 00:46:41.716
&gt;&gt; So I always make the assumption
that when people are doing something

00:46:41.716 --> 00:46:44.429
distracting like looking at their phone,

00:46:44.429 --> 00:46:47.988
that there is a important
reason that they are doing so.

00:46:47.988 --> 00:46:52.339
So I start from a place of compassion
rather than it would just hurt my ego

00:46:52.339 --> 00:46:54.891
too much to be thinking that I'm boring.

00:46:54.891 --> 00:46:55.963
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:46:55.963 --> 00:46:59.749
what I try to do is I try
to engage nonverbally.

00:46:59.749 --> 00:47:03.974
If you're standing up you can move closer
to the people who are distracted and

00:47:03.974 --> 00:47:08.134
we've all learned that as the voice
gets louder, we should pay attention.

00:47:08.134 --> 00:47:11.633
Try to bring them in asking
questions works well.

00:47:11.633 --> 00:47:15.167
What I have learned in the digital
digital age that I teach in and

00:47:15.167 --> 00:47:18.317
all my students have not just one but
multiple devices.

00:47:18.317 --> 00:47:21.634
Sometimes, you can then actually
enlist the devices in what you do.

00:47:21.634 --> 00:47:25.375
So you can ask people, go search on this
or there are tools that allow you to take

00:47:25.375 --> 00:47:28.168
polls through texting, so
people text a certain number.

00:47:28.168 --> 00:47:29.178
And that's a poll.

00:47:29.178 --> 00:47:30.760
And you can actually show the data live.

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:34.720
So rather than fight the technology,
see if there are ways that

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:38.092
you can leverage it to help
you accomplish your goal.

00:47:38.092 --> 00:47:40.003
And that will make you feel better.

00:47:40.003 --> 00:47:44.079
And it will actually lead to some very
interesting interactions, thank you,

00:47:44.079 --> 00:47:44.840
yes please.

00:47:44.840 --> 00:47:46.042
You don't have a mic, a mic is coming.

00:47:46.042 --> 00:47:48.888
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do you ever take into account the kind

00:47:48.888 --> 00:47:51.268
of the company's culture
when you're giving for

00:47:51.268 --> 00:47:53.818
example the founder of Trader Joe's,
Joe Coulomb,

00:47:53.818 --> 00:47:57.119
said that in all his messages he
wanted to have fun with the customer.

00:47:57.119 --> 00:48:00.550
As you give these presentations
do you take into account

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:03.108
Kind of what drives the company and
have that in your messaging.

00:48:03.108 --> 00:48:05.450
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, so
I try to practice what I preach.

00:48:05.450 --> 00:48:09.211
So when I go in and do some consulting
with companies all around the world,

00:48:09.211 --> 00:48:12.381
I spend time trying to understand
the culture and the values.

00:48:12.381 --> 00:48:15.238
And I watch some of the senior
leaders to see what they do.

00:48:15.238 --> 00:48:18.596
And then I infuse that in
the advice that I give them, and

00:48:18.596 --> 00:48:20.468
that can be really important.

00:48:20.468 --> 00:48:26.116
It could also get me in trouble, I went to
one start up not too far from here, and

00:48:26.116 --> 00:48:31.268
the executive team had a tradition
of cursing in their presentations.

00:48:31.268 --> 00:48:34.787
So, we had some interesting
examples in the workshop I led.

00:48:34.787 --> 00:48:38.905
But understanding what they do and
how they do it is very important for

00:48:38.905 --> 00:48:40.254
sure, yes, please.

00:48:40.254 --> 00:48:41.783
A microphone is coming your way.

00:48:44.489 --> 00:48:45.924
Thank you, it was great.

00:48:45.924 --> 00:48:48.712
I use my mobile for sharing the slides-
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:48:48.712 --> 00:48:49.590
&gt;&gt; To the group.

00:48:49.590 --> 00:48:50.827
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; So it was a good reason.

00:48:50.827 --> 00:48:55.099
&gt;&gt; Thank you, I have to tell you this
taking pictures of slides is new and

00:48:55.099 --> 00:48:56.877
I never know where to stand.

00:48:56.877 --> 00:48:59.366
I always feel I am
photobombing my materials.

00:48:59.366 --> 00:49:01.605
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so I am getting used to it but

00:49:01.605 --> 00:49:02.504
yes Nicholas.

00:49:02.504 --> 00:49:05.147
I can tell you you're going
to love your pictures.

00:49:05.147 --> 00:49:09.223
&gt;&gt; Excellent-
&gt;&gt; You look great, you look great.

00:49:09.223 --> 00:49:10.461
And now, I'm blanking.

00:49:10.461 --> 00:49:12.872
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Ask me a question.

00:49:12.872 --> 00:49:18.487
&gt;&gt; I know, I'm blanking on
asking you a question so I mean.

00:49:18.487 --> 00:49:23.006
[LAUGH] So I think it was about
what do you do with interrupts?

00:49:23.006 --> 00:49:27.088
So sometimes you're presenting,
and you get asked questions.

00:49:27.088 --> 00:49:27.725
&gt;&gt; What do you mean?

00:49:27.725 --> 00:49:30.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, how do you handle-

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:31.981
&gt;&gt; Samiar is a joker.

00:49:31.981 --> 00:49:32.492
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:49:32.492 --> 00:49:34.321
&gt;&gt; And a perfect interrupter.

00:49:34.321 --> 00:49:37.465
So what do you do when
something interrupts your flow?

00:49:37.465 --> 00:49:37.992
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:49:37.992 --> 00:49:40.019
So if you can ignore it, ignore it.

00:49:40.019 --> 00:49:44.884
If it's somebody cellphone going off or
something of that nature, ignore it.

00:49:44.884 --> 00:49:48.892
If it's somebody interrupting with
a question or some kind of comment,

00:49:48.892 --> 00:49:53.097
what I recommend you do, is you use
paraphrasing to take something from what

00:49:53.097 --> 00:49:55.151
they're saying and then redirect.

00:49:55.151 --> 00:49:57.292
So if somebody is saying, but,
what about this, what about this?

00:49:57.292 --> 00:49:59.728
You can say aah that's an important point.

00:49:59.728 --> 00:50:03.090
I'm really interested in, and
notice what I've just done.

00:50:03.090 --> 00:50:05.444
I acknowledged, and then I move away.

00:50:05.444 --> 00:50:09.256
I've signaled I'm done
with you non verbally.

00:50:09.256 --> 00:50:13.644
Here's the challenge when you
are under some kind of interrupt or

00:50:13.644 --> 00:50:18.354
somebody's challenging you,
you have to balance being appropriate

00:50:18.354 --> 00:50:23.169
versus being rude to the individual,
but also to the rest of the group.

00:50:23.169 --> 00:50:28.006
If somebody is being distracting and
interrupting and disrupting, the rest of

00:50:28.006 --> 00:50:33.004
the room is feeling that tension and very
rarely will the rest of the room help you.

00:50:33.004 --> 00:50:35.372
They're looking for you to deal with it.

00:50:35.372 --> 00:50:39.563
And if you don't then your
credibility is affected.

00:50:39.563 --> 00:50:42.932
So you have to understand that
you need to manage that and

00:50:42.932 --> 00:50:47.304
manage it as politely as you can and
I firmly believe that paraphrasing,

00:50:47.304 --> 00:50:51.912
taking something of value, and then
redirecting is the way to handle that.

00:50:51.912 --> 00:50:54.289
I'm going to take one more
question to stay on time but

00:50:54.289 --> 00:50:56.850
I will stay afterwards and
ask individual questions.

00:50:56.850 --> 00:50:59.020
Is there a final question?

00:50:59.020 --> 00:51:00.391
Right over here thank you.

00:51:03.583 --> 00:51:08.961
Thank you, my question is how do you
change track in a conversation or

00:51:08.961 --> 00:51:11.560
rather engaging conversation,

00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:16.571
especially when your counter
party is having this intense, and

00:51:16.571 --> 00:51:22.909
a quite tight logic connecting statements,
it's one question after another.

00:51:22.909 --> 00:51:25.651
But meanwhile, you're well aware,

00:51:25.651 --> 00:51:30.029
the conversation is just going
downward against your goal.

00:51:30.029 --> 00:51:32.081
&gt;&gt; Or not going where you want it to,
right.

00:51:32.081 --> 00:51:34.326
So how do you reguide the conversation?

00:51:34.326 --> 00:51:37.320
And again I don't mean
to sound repetitive, but

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:41.346
I really think paraphrasing is
a very polite tool for doing that.

00:51:41.346 --> 00:51:43.502
Acknowledge that you hear
what the person is saying.

00:51:43.502 --> 00:51:45.817
I understand what you're saying
right here, where that's going.

00:51:45.817 --> 00:51:48.809
I'm curios to know how so
use it as a bridge.

00:51:48.809 --> 00:51:51.263
So the paraphrase acknowledges, and

00:51:51.263 --> 00:51:54.415
then bridge to the direction
that you wish to go.

00:51:54.415 --> 00:51:58.551
I believe paraphrasing when paired
with structure and having a goal,

00:51:58.551 --> 00:52:02.555
are the three essential ingredients
to effective communication.

00:52:02.555 --> 00:52:03.926
Those are the bottom line tools.

00:52:03.926 --> 00:52:07.908
If you can create goals,
structure your messages and

00:52:07.908 --> 00:52:12.168
know how to paraphrase the content,
it can really help.

00:52:12.168 --> 00:52:15.903
I would like simply to
conclude by saying thank you.

00:52:15.903 --> 00:52:21.163
Each of us has important ideas and
stories and messages to convey.

00:52:21.163 --> 00:52:25.489
We must take the time to make sure,
that they are memorable for us so

00:52:25.489 --> 00:52:30.052
we can communicate them clearly,
and memorable for our audience, so

00:52:30.052 --> 00:52:32.012
they can take them with them.

00:52:32.012 --> 00:52:35.958
With that, I hope you are taking away some
memorable ideas to help you be a more

00:52:35.958 --> 00:52:37.370
effective communicator.

00:52:37.370 --> 00:52:38.563
Thank you very much.

00:52:38.563 --> 00:52:39.499
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:52:39.499 --> 00:52:44.485
[MUSIC]

